NOTE whisper small.en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:06.000
It's not always easy to combine all the things you care about into a single row number evaluation metric.

00:00:06.000 --> 00:00:12.000
In those cases, I found it sometimes useful to set up satisficing as well as optimizing metrics.

00:00:12.000 --> 00:00:14.000
Let me show you what I mean.

00:00:14.000 --> 00:00:20.000
Let's say that you've decided you care about the classification accuracy of your cat classifier.

00:00:20.000 --> 00:00:25.000
This could have been F1 score or some other measure of accuracy.

00:00:25.000 --> 00:00:29.000
But let's say that in addition to accuracy, you also care about the running time.

00:00:29.000 --> 00:00:32.000
So how long it takes to classify an image.

00:00:32.000 --> 00:00:39.000
And classifier A takes 80 milliseconds, B takes 95 milliseconds, and C takes 1,500 milliseconds.

00:00:39.000 --> 00:00:42.000
That's 1.5 seconds to classify an image.

00:00:42.000 --> 00:00:48.000
So one thing you could do is combine accuracy and running time into an overall evaluation metric.

00:00:48.000 --> 00:00:58.000
And so the cost, such as maybe the overall cost, is accuracy minus 0.5 times running time.

00:00:58.000 --> 00:01:05.000
But maybe it seems a bit artificial to combine accuracy and running time using a formula like this,

00:01:05.000 --> 00:01:08.000
like a linear weighted sum of these two things.

00:01:08.000 --> 00:01:13.000
So here's something else you could do instead, which is that you might want to choose a classifier

00:01:13.000 --> 00:01:22.340
that maximizes accuracy, but subject to that the running time,

00:01:22.340 --> 00:01:33.540
that is the time it takes to classify an image, that that has to be less than or equal to 100 milliseconds.

00:01:33.540 --> 00:01:41.690
So in this case, we would say that accuracy is an optimizing metric,

00:01:41.690 --> 00:01:47.690
because you want to maximize accuracy. You want to do as well as possible on accuracy.

00:01:47.690 --> 00:01:53.690
But that running time is what we call a satisficing metric,

00:01:53.690 --> 00:01:57.690
meaning that it just has to be good enough. It just needs to be less than 100 milliseconds.

00:01:57.690 --> 00:02:03.690
And beyond that, you don't really care. Or at least you don't care that much.

00:02:03.690 --> 00:02:11.690
So this would be a pretty reasonable way to trade off or to put together accuracy as well as running time.

00:02:11.690 --> 00:02:15.690
And it may be the case that so long as the running time is less than 100 milliseconds,

00:02:15.690 --> 00:02:21.690
your users won't care that much, whether it's 100 milliseconds or 50 milliseconds or even faster.

00:02:21.690 --> 00:02:29.690
And by defining, optimizing as well as satisfying metrics, this gives you a clear way to pick the quote, best classifier,

00:02:29.690 --> 00:02:36.690
which in this case would be classified B because of all the ones with a running time better than 100 milliseconds,

00:02:36.690 --> 00:02:45.690
it has the best accuracy. So more generally, if you have n metrics that you care about,

00:02:45.690 --> 00:02:53.690
it's sometimes reasonable to pick one of them to be optimizing. So you want to do as well as possible on that one

00:02:53.690 --> 00:02:59.690
and then n minus one to be satisficing, meaning that so long as they reach some threshold,

00:02:59.690 --> 00:03:03.690
such as running times faster than 100 milliseconds, but as long as they reach some threshold,

00:03:03.690 --> 00:03:08.690
you don't care how much better it is in that threshold, but they have to reach that threshold.

00:03:09.690 --> 00:03:18.690
Here's another example. Let's say you're building a system to detect wake words, also called trigger words.

00:03:18.690 --> 00:03:26.690
So this refers to the voice control devices, like the Amazon Echo, which you wake up by saying Alexa,

00:03:26.690 --> 00:03:32.690
or some Google devices, which wake up by saying, OK, Google, or some Apple devices,

00:03:32.690 --> 00:03:39.690
which wake up by saying, hey Siri, or some Baidu devices, which wake up by saying, you know,

00:03:39.690 --> 00:03:47.690
Ni hao Baidu, or I guess you already Chinese, that's Ni hao Baidu.

00:03:47.690 --> 00:03:54.690
So these are the wake words you use to tell one of these voice control devices to wake up

00:03:54.690 --> 00:04:00.690
and listen to something you want to say. But these are the Chinese characters for Ni hao Baidu.

00:04:00.690 --> 00:04:08.000
So you might care about the accuracy of your trigger word detection system.

00:04:08.000 --> 00:04:13.000
So when someone says one of these trigger words, how likely are you to actually wake up your device?

00:04:13.000 --> 00:04:17.000
And you might also care about the number of false positives.

00:04:17.000 --> 00:04:22.000
So when no one actually said this trigger word, how often does it randomly wake up?

00:04:23.000 --> 00:04:32.000
So in this case, maybe one reasonable way of combining these two evaluation metrics might be to maximize accuracy.

00:04:32.000 --> 00:04:37.000
So when someone says one of the trigger words, maximize the chance that your device wakes up,

00:04:37.000 --> 00:04:50.430
and subject to that you have at most one false positive, you know, every 24 hours of operation.

00:04:50.430 --> 00:04:56.430
So that your device randomly wakes up only once per day on average when no one is actually talking to it.

00:04:56.430 --> 00:05:01.430
So in this case, accuracy is the optimizing metric.

00:05:01.430 --> 00:05:08.430
And the number of false positives every 24 hours is the satisfying metric where you be satisfied

00:05:08.430 --> 00:05:14.430
so long as there's at most one false positive every 24 hours.

00:05:14.430 --> 00:05:17.430
To summarize, if there are multiple things you care about,

00:05:17.430 --> 00:05:21.430
by setting up one as the optimizing metric that you want to do as well as possible on,

00:05:21.430 --> 00:05:27.430
and one or more as satisfying metrics where you be satisfied so long as it does better than some threshold,

00:05:27.430 --> 00:05:33.430
you can now have an automatic way, almost automatic way of quickly looking at multiple classifiers

00:05:33.430 --> 00:05:35.430
and picking the quote, best one.

00:05:35.430 --> 00:05:42.430
Now, these evaluation metrics must be evaluated or calculated on a training set or a development set

00:05:42.430 --> 00:05:43.430
or maybe on a test set.

00:05:43.430 --> 00:05:49.430
So one of the things you also need to do is set up training dev or development as well as test sets.

00:05:49.430 --> 00:05:55.430
In the next video, I want to share with you some guidelines for how to set up training dev and test sets.

00:05:55.430 --> 00:05:57.430
So let's go on to the next video.
